<article class="env-lemma" id="0AME">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AME">Lemma <span data-tag="0AME">10.19.1</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring with Jacobson radical $\text{rad}(R)$. Let $I \subset R$ be an ideal. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$I \subset \text{rad}(R)$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
every element of $1 + I$ is a unit in $R$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> In this case every element of $R$ which maps to a unit of $R/I$ is a unit. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $f \in \text{rad}(R)$, then $f \in \mathfrak m$ for all maximal ideals $\mathfrak m$ of $R$. Hence $1 + f \not\in \mathfrak m$ for all maximal ideals $\mathfrak m$ of $R$. Thus the closed subset $V(1 + f)$ of $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$ is empty. This implies that $1 + f$ is a unit, see Lemma <a href="/tag/00E0" data-tag="00E0">10.17.2</a>. 
    <p>
      Conversely, assume that $1 + f$ is a unit for all $f \in I$. If $\mathfrak m$ is a maximal ideal and $I \not\subset \mathfrak m$, then $I + \mathfrak m = R$. Hence $1 = f + g$ for some $g \in \mathfrak m$ and $f \in I$. Then $g = 1 + (-f)$ is not a unit, contradiction. 
    <p>
      For the final statement let $f \in R$ map to a unit in $R/I$. Then we can find $g \in R$ mapping to the multiplicative inverse of $f \bmod I$. Then $fg = 1 \bmod I$. Hence $fg$ is a unit of $R$ by (2) which implies that $f$ is a unit. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>